  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe surface high pressure to our north that has been sponsoring \nour dry days and comfortable afternoon humidity levels will \ncontinue to extend into our area as it drifts slowly east this \nafternoon and tonight. Moisture will begin to increase Wednesday \nbut most areas will remain rain free, with just some isolated to \nscattered afternoon convection expected especially over the \neastern mountains. \n\nPrecipitation chances will increase for Thursday into Friday as \nmultiple weak short waves move across and a weak surface front moves \nslowly toward and then into our area.  Models have been struggling a \nlot with the details of these features, but right now it appears the \nfront will push into our area Thursday night and is unlikely to move \noff to our to south and east until sometime Friday.  Latest runs \nsuggest short wave energy ahead of the front may have convection \nmoving in during the early morning hours Thursday morning, and if \nthis occurs clouds and precipitation could limit instability during \nthe day Thursday. CAPE values currently look modest with limited \nshear Thursday into Friday, so the potential for organized severe \nstorms still looks low. However, a few storms may produce strong \ngusty winds either day and locally heavy rainfall will also be \npossible.\n\n\nUpper ridging and surface high pressure will build in by the \nweekend, with most areas expected to be dry. The NBM just has some \nlow chances for afternoon showers and storms mainly in the mountains \nboth Saturday and Sunday, with the limited precip chances continuing \ninto the first part of the week. We will also see a gradual warming \ntrend during the weekend into the first part of the week, with high \ntemperatures likely to be in the 90s across most valley locations by \nTuesday.",
